><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20251013092630+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="c112fc49-12c3-4da9-80d6-b3dd63f223df" date="D:20251013092708+10'00'" name="ffbac832-9e7b-4b46-8a73-f0e46682ffd0" icon="Comment" page="8" rect="379.671631,152.744614,403.671631,176.744614" subject="Sticky Note" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Clinicians working with professional ballet&#xD;dancers should consider bone stress&#xD;as a differential diagnosis in instances&#xD;of tibial, ankle, or foot pain. Early identification of BSIs may reduce time-loss&#xD;burden. Steps should be taken during&#xD;rehabilitation to prevent recurrence of&#xD;BSI, considering factors that influence&#xD;bone health, as well as management of&#xD;training load.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="8" rect="576.000000,84.744629,760.000000,176.744629"
/></text
